  <h1>How Pregnancy Discrimination Damages Work</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Riley Chen (RC), Employment &amp; Civil Rights Practice Lead. Updated May 2026.</em></p>
  <h2>Back pay</h2><p>All wages and benefits lost from the date of the discriminatory act through judgment. Uncapped under Title VII. Plaintiff must mitigate by seeking comparable work; failure to mitigate reduces back pay by the amount that could have been earned.</p>
  <h2>Compensatory and punitive damages (capped)</h2><p>Combined cap by employer size under 42 U.S.C. § 1981a: $50K (15–100 employees), $100K (101–200), $200K (201–500), $300K (500+). Compensatory covers emotional distress, humiliation, and loss of enjoyment of life. Punitive requires malice or reckless indifference.</p>
  <h2>Front pay</h2><p>Future lost earnings when reinstatement is not feasible. Equitable remedy — awarded by judge, not jury. Not subject to the Title VII cap. Duration depends on plaintiff&rsquo;s age, job market, and likelihood of equivalent employment.</p>
  <h2>PWFA remedies</h2><p>The PWFA added explicit reasonable accommodation requirements effective June 2023. Remedies for PWFA violations include the same Title VII remedies: back pay, compensatory/punitive damages (within caps), front pay, and attorney fees.</p>
  <h2>Where to go from here</h2>
  <p>See <a href="/guides/types-of-pregnancy-discrimination">types of discrimination</a> and <a href="/guides/what-to-do-after-pregnancy-discrimination">what to do after discrimination</a>.</p>
